# Commission: learning a governing rule

## Intended contribution
Help the designer of a wiki-based agent or another learning system distinguish learning new evidence under unchanged rules from acquiring or revising an instruction, check, or routing rule governing future decisions. The latter, not mere history-dependent outputs or storage versus activation, is the target. Show how to tell for an actual system.

## Must keep
Present one worked incident with competing interpretations: past incident as advice, changed standing reviewer prompt, or changed validator that can block acceptance. Name consumer/channel/force, what changed in the rule or its scope, and the consequence when old and new rules disagree. Distinguish effective use from merely saving policy.md, and instruction from guaranteed enforcement. A rule can change with the same channel and force. Separate demonstrating a changed rule, showing deployment experience led to it, and evidence it helps. Keep the universal-machine objection in its strongest form: arbitrary learned code is data to a fixed universal interpreter; even a nearly executable validator needing an LLM to add a wrapper is not classified by executable syntax. Our practical distinction is how the deployed system treats it. No named external evidence needed for these hypothetical examples.

## Must exclude
Do not define all learning or continual learning by revising governing rules. Do not redescribe all history-sensitive computation as governance, equate data with advice, or require a reject-capable gate, human exclusion, or self-modifying interpreter. Do not imply a general fixed reviewer with access to advisory history cannot match a revised reviewer. No empirical literature claims, unsourced regression generalizations, or broad architecture catalogue. Incoming uses that lose warrant are consequences to report, not constraints to preserve.

## Reserved decisions
Single candidate only, no publication, relocation, sibling edits, write brief or second note. Preserve title as workflow requires; explicitly flag title mismatch in report rather than distorting claim to defend it. Final retitle is a separate operator decision.

## Current direction
Rebuild around acquiring governing rules. Keep necessity under open-ended conditions only as a focused open question in this note: under what workload, time/reliability constraints or consequence would consulted experience not suffice? A stronger fixed-reviewer comparison is welcome, an impossibility claim is not. Prefer a concise note with a specific diagnostic and an honest limit. Do not repeat the previous trial's matched-history thesis or general storage discussion. Use library sources for the actual meaning of authority; the gate example only illustrates a rule scope, not an observed learning episode. Check candidate description length and mark uncertain dependent uses accurately.
